### NoSQL > Neo4j > Spring Data Neo4j Repositories

Source: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/blob/v4.0.0/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/reference/pages/data/nosql.adoc

In an application using the reactive style with Neo4j, a `ReactiveTransactionManager` is not auto-configured by default. To enable transaction management in a reactive application, you must explicitly define the transaction manager bean in your configuration. This ensures that reactive transactions are properly managed when using reactive Neo4j repositories and templates.

--------------------------------

### Distributed Transactions With JTA

Source: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/blob/v4.0.0/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/reference/pages/io/jta.adoc

Spring Boot supports distributed JTA transactions across multiple XA resources by using a transaction manager retrieved from JNDI. When a JTA environment is detected, Spring's javadoc:org.springframework.transaction.jta.JtaTransactionManager[] is used to manage transactions. Auto-configured JMS, DataSource, and JPA beans are upgraded to support XA transactions. You can use standard Spring idioms, such as javadoc:org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional[format=annotation], to participate in a distributed transaction. If you are within a JTA environment and still want to use local transactions, you can set the configprop:spring.jta.enabled[] property to `false` to disable the JTA auto-configuration.

--------------------------------

### Disable Transaction Management in JDBC Tests

Source: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/blob/v4.0.0/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/reference/pages/testing/spring-boot-applications.adoc

Demonstrates how to disable automatic transaction management for JDBC tests using @JdbcTest. By default, JDBC tests are transactional and roll back after each test; this shows how to override that behavior.

```java
@JdbcTest
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)
public class MyTransactionalTests {
  @Autowired
  private JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate;

  @Test
  public void testWithoutTransactions() {
    // Test methods here
  }
}
```

--------------------------------

### Testing > Auto-configured Data JDBC Tests > Transaction Management

Source: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/blob/v4.0.0/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/reference/pages/testing/spring-boot-applications.adoc

Data JDBC tests are transactional by default and automatically roll back at the end of each test. This transactional behavior can be disabled for individual tests or entire test classes if needed. If you prefer your test to run against a real database instead of an embedded one, you can use the `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase` annotation to override the default in-memory database configuration.

--------------------------------

### Batch Applications > Specifying a Batch Transaction Manager

Source: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/blob/v4.0.0/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/how-to/pages/batch.adoc

Similar to xref:batch.adoc#howto.batch.specifying-a-data-source[], you can define a javadoc:org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager[] for use in batch processing by annotating its javadoc:org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean[format=annotation] method with javadoc:org.springframework.boot.batch.autoconfigure.BatchTransactionManager[format=annotation]. If you do so and want two transaction managers (for example by retaining the auto-configured javadoc:org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager[]), set the `defaultCandidate` attribute of the javadoc:org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean[format=annotation] annotation to `false`.

